In the steamy heat of Stranglethorn Vale, Pip Sprocketspark, a gnome warlock, trudged through the underbrush, his succubus, Velvet, gliding beside him. A panther stalked them. Velvet blew a kiss—the panther froze, dazed, and Pip’s shadowbolts tore it apart. “See?” she purred. “Charm beats a meat shield.” Pip grinned, but then a rogue ambushed him from behind. Velvet’s charm broke as she turned, but the rogue’s stun locked Pip down. Velvet’s seduction had a cooldown—she couldn’t re-charm instantly. Pip died. Resurrecting, he sighed. “That’s why everyone uses voidwalkers,” he muttered. “Sacrifice and taunt don’t have a cooldown on saving your skin.”

**Answer:** The succubus’s *Seduction* is a crowd-control skill, but it only works on humanoids and breaks on damage. In classic, most warlocks leveling in contested zones (like STV) face many non-humanoid mobs (beasts, elementals) and frequent PvP ganks. The voidwalker’s taunt and sacrifice are reliable, passive survivability, while seduction is situational and easily broken—so despite succubus’s higher DPS, the voidwalker’s consistency makes it the perceived “default.” [end of text]


